# Algebraic connectivity and the graph Laplacian, the spectral measure of how integrated a network is. The Laplacian of a
# graph is the degree matrix minus the adjacency matrix. It always has the eigenvalue zero from the constant vector, and
# the second-smallest eigenvalue is the Fiedler value, the algebraic connectivity, positive exactly when the graph is
# connected and larger the harder the graph is to cut. This is the quantity an integration measure reaches for, the
# spectral gap that says the whole exceeds its separable parts. The complete graph on four nodes is the extreme case, its
# nodes fully interchangeable under a cyclic automorphism. This file MODELS the four nodes and DEFINES the cyclic rotation
# that permutes them, then PROVES it has order four, a symmetry of the complete graph. The Laplacian trace as the sum of
# the degrees and the spanning-tree count by Cayley's formula are recorded as machine-integer facts. The order theorem is
# proved over the actual node type by induction.

# The four nodes of the complete graph, permuted by its cyclic automorphism.
form node
  case node-a
  case node-b
  case node-c
  case node-d

# The cyclic automorphism of the complete graph, rotating its four interchangeable nodes.
task rotate
  take x, like node
  like node
  fork case, read x
    case node-a
      send back
        make node-b
    case node-b
      send back
        make node-c
    case node-c
      send back
        make node-d
    case node-d
      send back
        make node-a

# THE CYCLIC AUTOMORPHISM HAS ORDER FOUR: four rotations return every node, rotate applied four times is the identity, for
# every node, a symmetry of the complete graph on four nodes whose vertices are fully interchangeable. Proved by induction
# over the four nodes.
rule the-cyclic-automorphism-has-order-four
  seat x, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call rotate
        call rotate
          call rotate
            call rotate
              read x
      read x
  fold x

# THE LAPLACIAN TRACE is the sum of the degrees, twice the edge count: the complete graph on four nodes has every node of
# degree three, so the trace is 4 * 3 = 12, which is also the sum of the eigenvalues zero, four, four, four.
hold laplacian-trace-is-the-degree-sum
  call is-equal
    code 12
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 3

# THE SPANNING-TREE COUNT is Cayley's formula, n to the n minus two: the complete graph on four nodes has 4 * 4 = 16
# spanning trees, four to the second.
hold spanning-tree-count-is-cayley
  call is-equal
    code 16
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 4
